WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.300
How can one activated molecule change a whole cell's current without letting its

2
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.340
signal spread or run forever?

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.580
Follow one photon into a rod cell.

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.580
Its pathway reveals both the power and the cost of amplification.

5
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:25.293
In darkness, cGMP holds channels open, and ions carry a steady dark current.

6
00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:28.733
The photon activates one rhodopsin.

7
00:00:29.913 --> 00:00:31.073
It does not add current.

8
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:36.473
It starts a relay that closes some channels, so the existing current dips.

9
00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:40.667
Active rhodopsin switches on many transducins.

10
00:00:41.947 --> 00:00:47.427
Each active transducin activates phosphodiesterase, which destroys cGMP.

11
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.600
As cGMP falls, some channels close.

12
00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:56.320
The current dip is only a small fraction of the dark current.

13
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.200
One event has become many downstream events.

14
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.640
Gain is output events per input event.

15
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.880
In a simple unsaturated cascade, total gain is the product of the step gains.

16
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:13.073
A catalytic step multiplies.

17
00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:17.153
A one-to-one handoff contributes no multiplication.

18
00:01:18.613 --> 00:01:22.773
The transducin handoff is one-to-one, between two catalytic stages.

19
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:30.407
A catalytic molecule reaches more targets by working faster or remaining active

20
00:01:30.407 --> 00:01:35.167
longer. Rate times active lifetime helps set that step's gain.

21
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.170
Longer activity can also prolong output.

22
00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:44.130
But downstream shutoff or feedback can shorten a high-gain output.

23
00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.710
Size and duration are not locked together.

24
00:01:48.783 --> 00:01:51.323
Now compare two invented routes on screen.

25
00:01:52.643 --> 00:01:54.123
Both start from one input.

26
00:01:55.423 --> 00:01:57.443
Which route produces more final output?

27
00:01:58.763 --> 00:02:02.643
Follow every branch, then multiply each route's displayed step gains.

28
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:13.330
The upper route wins because its displayed factors give the larger product.

29
00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:15.730
This rule has limits.

30
00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:22.653
Saturation, shared substrates, deactivation, and feedback can make real pathways

31
00:02:22.653 --> 00:02:24.393
depart from simple multiplication.

32
00:02:25.933 --> 00:02:29.193
Use the product only when the simple counting assumptions fit.

33
00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:35.927
Amplification also magnifies some upstream fluctuations, so weak signals need

34
00:02:35.927 --> 00:02:42.567
evidence. Rod researchers separated blank trials from responses and tested their

35
00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:44.487
counts against a Poisson distribution.

36
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.960
Every active component needs a stop.

37
00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.500
Rhodopsin is shut down, and transducin splits bound GTP to switch itself off.

38
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.840
A diffusible messenger can also reach unintended targets.

39
00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:04.940
Local binding, anchoring, and destruction restrict its useful pool.

40
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:11.760
Anchoring proteins can hold a kinase, its target, and a messenger-destroying enzyme

41
00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:12.780
within one neighbourhood.

42
00:03:13.883 --> 00:03:16.423
Amplification is therefore not free.

43
00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:22.523
A strong signal must be stopped, and a messenger that spreads may need to be kept

44
00:03:22.523 --> 00:03:23.543
near the right targets.

45
00:03:24.633 --> 00:03:26.013
Return to the photon.

46
00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:32.613
Its cascade made a detectable dip because catalytic stages multiplied its effect,

47
00:03:33.153 --> 00:03:35.233
and timed shutoff ended the response.

48
00:03:36.593 --> 00:03:41.773
In other pathways, local anchoring and destruction can also limit the spread of a

49
00:03:41.773 --> 00:03:42.853
diffusible messenger.

50
00:03:44.173 --> 00:03:46.613
Those safeguards make amplification useful.
